$PROGRAM-NAME   fat_font$VERSION        v1.0$PURPOSE        Special font$SUMMARY        This font uses two pixels for a line and makes                a massive impression. Good for presentations.                The font is a sans-serif; expanded ASCII has                frame-drawing and Greek glyphs.$KEYWORDS       font, helvetica, sans-serif, ascii, *stuff, mono$STATUS         final$REFERENCES     ptob, btop      for textfile generation and compilation,                                see below, under "miscellaneous"                p_convert_lq    corresponding printer driver$HARDWARE       requires mono card and VGA-type monitor$AUTHOR-NAME    Otto E. Tutzauer$AVAILABILITY   Public$DISTRIBUTOR    EFFO, CoVe$HISTORY        -$EXPANSION      expansion to the other character sets,                character generator and font downloader for printer                (I also have a '386 on the same printer, so I use                an MS-DOS PD font downloader, then switch the printer                over to the OS-9 machine)$INSTALLATION   1.  For an Atari or compatible monitor                    - copy fat_font to boot floppy                    - rename monostuff monostuff.old                    - rename fat_font monostuff                    - boot the machine                2.  For a different monochrome monitor                    - merge fat_fontset with your gepterm_*                    - correct CRC with fixmod -u                    - rename it *stuff                    - continue as described under 1.$BUGS           none known$RESTRICTIONS   All other character sets except font#1 are unchanged$MISCELLANEOUS  This font has been generated as follows:                btop converts an existing font to a text file (size                is 262112 bytes). This text file is then edited with                emacs (very carefully, make many backups, do not                change the size) and compiled with btop.                The fontface file is then merged with the adequate                gepterm_* code and named monostuff. The CRC is                corrected with fixmod -u.                The graphic and scientific glyphs do not correspond                to the MS-DOS standard; when transferring texts to                such machines, use emacs on the MS-DOS machine and                "execute-file" a file containing the replace charac-                ters (g2i) on that machine.                The author of btop and ptob is Detlef Lammermann.                I will be pleased to hear from you if you want to                sink time into a character set generation.